    {
      "id": "GENUS_EURYPOGON",
      "name": "Eurypogon",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "lineage": "Eurypogon",
      "description": "Eurypogon — a genus in the family Artematopodidae.",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "SP_EURYPOGON_BREVIPENNIS",
          "name": "Eurypogon brevipennis",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "lineage": "Eurypogon",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "description": "This species can be easily recognized based on the pronotum with sides nearly straight in both sexes (Fig. 8 F), the relatively short elytra (Fig. 4 C) which are together 1.8 – 1.9 times as long as wide, the relatively smooth elytral surface (Fig. 10 I) with mostly large punctures, and a unique shape of the aedeagal median lobe which is distinctly broad basally, gradually narrowed medially, and then slightly widened subapically (Sakai 1982). Sexual dimorphism. Females are slightly more robust and relatively shorter than males. For more information, see Sakai (1982).",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:1162299",
          "authorship": "Sakai, 1982",
          "children": [],
          "sourceIds": {
            "gbif": [
              "1162299"
            ],
            "itis": [
              "708634"
            ],
            "checklistbank": "1489355"
          },
          "uuid": "d7993b3a-0c84-5ca-9c0-ec3a5170266ca4",
          "authorityIds": "gbif:1162299,itis:708634"
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_EURYPOGON_CALIFORNICUS",
          "name": "Eurypogon californicus",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "lineage": "Eurypogon",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "description": "Eurypogon californicus is a species of soft-bodied plant beetle in the family Artematopodidae. It is found in North America.",
          "wikipediaTitle": "Eurypogon californicus",
          "continents": [
            "North America"
          ],
          "authorship": "Horn, 1880",
          "children": [],
          "sourceIds": {
            "gbif": [
              "1162301"
            ],
            "itis": [
              "708384"
            ],
            "checklistbank": "491622"
          },
          "uuid": "8f837666-baaa-50a-9d1-dd57e2f96764cc",
          "authorityIds": "gbif:1162301,itis:708384"
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_EURYPOGON_GRANULATUS",
          "name": "Eurypogon granulatus",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "lineage": "Eurypogon",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "description": "This species can be easily recognized based on the distinctly granulated rough surface of interstices between puncture rows on elytra (Fig. 10 G, H), relatively large elytral punctures, and slender, relatively thin median lobe (Figs 12 G). Sexual dimorphism and variability. Males are relatively narrower and more elongate than females, being about 2.8 times as long as wide (excluding head), while females are more robust, being about 2.4 times as long as wide (excluding head; Fig. 4 A, B). Males have longer and more serrate antennae, and less robust and relatively slightly shorter pronotum (Fig. 8 D, E). For more information, see Sakai (1982). We found the difference between a male and a female in the relative size of punctures on elytra (Fig. 10 G, H); however, more specimens of both sexes should be studied to understand if this is a sexual dimorphism or intraspecific variability.",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:1162305",
          "authorship": "Sakai, 1982",
          "children": [],
          "sourceIds": {
            "gbif": [
              "1162305"
            ],
            "itis": [
              "708633"
            ]
          },
          "uuid": "53d27ccc-c385-5fe-b1c-507e4db3419cd5",
          "authorityIds": "gbif:1162305,itis:708633"
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_EURYPOGON_HARRISII",
          "name": "Eurypogon harrisii",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "lineage": "Eurypogon",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "description": "Eurypogon harrisii is a species of soft-bodied plant beetle in the family Artematopodidae. It is found in North America.",
          "wikipediaTitle": "Eurypogon harrisii",
          "continents": [
            "North America"
          ],
          "authorship": "(Westwood, 1862)",
          "children": [],
          "sourceIds": {
            "gbif": [
              "1162306"
            ],
            "itis": [
              "708386"
            ],
            "checklistbank": "1770639"
          },
          "uuid": "7bda2235-fb5e-55d-aa8-85d9851d243857",
          "authorityIds": "gbif:1162306,itis:708386"
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_EURYPOGON_HISAMATSUI",
          "name": "Eurypogon hisamatsui",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "lineage": "Eurypogon",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "description": "This species can be recognized based on the following combination of characters: punctures on head and pronotum (Figs 4 D, 9 A) relatively small and deep, elytra (Fig. 4 D) together more than twice as long as wide, elytral punctures (Fig. 10 J) fine, and aedeagal median lobe relatively broad for most of its length, then gradually narrowed towards apex (Sakai 1982). Sexual dimorphism. Females are slightly more robust and relatively shorter than males, with distinctly shorter antennae. For more information, see Sakai (1982).",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:1162304",
          "authorship": "Sakai, 1982",
          "children": [],
          "sourceIds": {
            "gbif": [
              "1162304"
            ],
            "itis": [
              "708635"
            ],
            "checklistbank": "1489356"
          },
          "uuid": "3fbfee97-00da-556-92c-a50e3a6923a433",
          "authorityIds": "gbif:1162304,itis:708635"
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_EURYPOGON_JAPONICUS",
          "name": "Eurypogon japonicus",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "lineage": "Eurypogon",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "description": "This species can be recognized based on the following combination of characters: eyes almost as wide as the anterior margin of pronotum (Figs 5 A, B; 9 B, C), pronotal punctation (Fig. 9 B, C) very dense, with most punctures very close to each other, elytra together about 2.3 times as long as wide, the elytral surface (Fig. 10 K) rather smooth and with mostly large punctures, and the median lobe (Fig. 12 H) about 5.4 – 5.8 times as long as wide. Sexual dimorphism. Females are more robust and relatively shorter than males, being about 2.4 – 2.5 times as long as wide (excluding head; about 2.7 times in males; Fig. 5 A, B). Females have shorter and less serrate antennae (serrations less acute) than males (Sakai 1982).",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:1162302",
          "authorship": "Sakai, 1982",
          "children": [],
          "sourceIds": {
            "gbif": [
              "1162302"
            ],
            "itis": [
              "708631"
            ],
            "checklistbank": "341954"
          },
          "uuid": "5e8dfa3e-e91a-58f-b50-dc4b61f51268b1",
          "authorityIds": "gbif:1162302,itis:708631"
        },
